Here is the provided data.

<fuzz-target>
#include <fuzzer/FuzzedDataProvider.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>

#include "/src/libevent/include/event2/http.h"
#include "/src/libevent/include/event2/http_compat.h"
#include "/src/libevent/include/event2/event.h"

extern "C" int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *data, size_t size) {
FuzzedDataProvider data_provider(data, size);

const std::string address = data_provider.ConsumeRandomLengthString();
const uint16_t port = data_provider.ConsumeIntegral<uint16_t>();

// Call the target function directly.
struct evhttp *http = evhttp_start(address.c_str(), port);

// If the server started successfully, free the resources.
if (http) {
evhttp_free(http);
}

return 0;
}
</fuzz-target>

<function-requirements>

<input>
project name: libevent
function signature: struct evhttp * evhttp_start(const char *address, uint16_t port)
</input>

<description>
The evhttp_start function initializes a new HTTP server instance. It creates a new evhttp object and then binds it to a socket on the specified address and port. The address can be an IP address or a hostname. If the address is NULL and the port is 0, an unbound socket is created. The function returns a pointer to the new evhttp structure on success, or NULL on failure.
</description>

<requirements>
<requirement>
The `address` parameter must be a valid, null-terminated string representing an IPv4 or IPv6 address, or a resolvable hostname. This is because the underlying `bind_socket` function uses `evutil_getaddrinfo` to resolve the address, and an invalid string will cause the address resolution to fail, leading to a NULL return from `evhttp_start`.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `port` parameter must be a valid TCP port number. This is because the port number is passed to the `bind` system call, and must be a value that the operating system can use to bind the socket.
</requirement>
</requirements>

</function-requirements>

